From: tribble@netcom.com (E. Dean Tribble)
Date: Wed, 9 Feb 1994 09:47:53 -0800
Subject: [#94-2-94] SEMANTICS:  Faith

At 12:09 PM 2/7/94 +0000, nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu wrote:
>What's tendon development? I thought that tendons were connective
>tissue, and that you couldn't do much with them other than stretching.
>
Tendons get stronger with exercise, just much slower than muscles do (I
recall they develop ~1/10th as fast).  The standing meditations, the slow
movements of Tai Chi Chuan, etc. all develop to tendon strength, among
other things.  Notice when next you see a good martial artist (particularly
Jui Jitsu, Aikido, or Tai Chi Chuan) how big their tendons are.  If you try
to press on the tendons connecting the upper to the lower arm, they are
extemely hard and thick, for instance.

Tendon strength has a great deal to do with effective strength in martial
arts, and it takes quite a while to develop.  This is an example of why
there aren't many shortcuts.

