Subject: [#94-2-99] SCI: Brain Wave Alteration

Recently I've been hearing about machines that help one alter one's
consciousness by affecting hir brain waves.  Apparently they involve
goggles and a sound generator, and can take one from beta to alpha to theta
(even delta?) state.

I've heard all kinds of miraculous things about these machines (mostly from
ad copy, which gives them a few grains of salt to begin with) -- needing
25% less sleep, learning languages twice as fast, increasing reading speed
and comprehension, curing neuroses, etc.  I might dismiss them out of hand,
except that it occurred to me that if I were as unfamiliar with the
operation of my body as I am about the operation of my mind, I'd consider
similar claims about the body just as outlandish (Run a mile in half the
time!  Lift your body weight!  Triple your endurance!  Kick higher than
your head!).

So I figured I'd ask people on the list:  have you had personal experience
with such a machine?  If so, what did you think of it (and do you have any
advice on how to use it)?  If not, do you know anybody who has?  Do you
have any scientific data on these machines?  Does the whole idea seem
patently foolish?  Is there anything similar but more scientifically valid
on the market?

Enquiring minds want to know,
Kennita


